# omclone
A runner of Opus Magnum solutions with a UI that will help long tape solves.

The app starts out with the paths in "src/default_paths.txt": the base folder, then the puzzle and the solution
(relative to it), one per line. That file is personal, so it's gitignored: copy "default_paths_SAMPLE.txt" to it and
edit in the path to your Opus Magnum puzzle/solution folder. Without one, the sample's paths are used.

Code is heavily influenced by omsim (https://github.com/ianh/omsim).


The default feature "editor_ui" builds the editor. "display_ui" on its own (--no-default-features --features display_ui)
builds a version that only shows solutions running, without editing.
You can use "cargo build --release --target wasm32-unknown-unknown" to build the js version. Example html pages are in display_html
You may want to change between opt-level "3" and "s" for executable vs. wasm respectively.

## Controls
In the app, the Help button in the Part window lists the keys and mouse controls for the board and the Part window,
and the "?" above the tapes lists the tapes'. Some things they don't explain:

Board and Part window
- At a later timestep, dragging an arm moves where it starts by the same amount, so a track may carry it somewhere
  else; while dragging, a faint copy shows how it starts.
- An arm's x, y, rot and length in the Part window are where it starts; the "Now" line shows where it is at the
  current timestep.

Timeline (the "World loaded" window)
- Run plays the timeline at Speed timesteps a frame.
- Loop length is how long looping tapes run before they start over.
- Collide/Area: Show and Hide check collisions and count area (Show also shades it). Off skips both, which is
  faster.
- A crash is circled in red on the board, and the Crash window's Show button centers the view on it.

File info window
- It notes anything omsim (and so the leaderboard) would reject the solution for, such as a part or instruction the
  puzzle disables, and what loading changed from the file (an out-of-range arm length, an unknown part or
  instruction, two instructions at one timestep), which Save writes as loaded.
----
Enable feature "color-eyre" for more detailed error reports. Load and simulation errors are shown in the app either way.

The ignored tests "check_all" and "check_all_rebuilt" run every solution under test/solution and
test/om-leaderboard-master against the puzzles under test/puzzle (each searched recursively, and matched by the
puzzle's file name). The test folder is gitignored, so fill it in yourself, then run "cargo test --lib -- --ignored".
Set OMCLONE_CHECK_AREA=1 to check area too (much slower).

The puzzle-specific variant checkers ("hab_checker" and "cx_checker") have been removed.
See CHECKERS.md for how they worked and what adding a new one would take.

## Verifier library
The "verify" folder builds a library with omsim's verifier API (omsim's verifier.h), so it can be loaded in place of
omsim's libverify, measuring with omclone's sim:
- Native: "cargo build -p omclone-verify --profile verify" makes target/verify/libverify.so on Linux, or verify.dll on
  Windows (omsim's is named libverify.dll there, so rename it).
- Web: "cargo build -p omclone-verify --release --target wasm32-unknown-unknown" makes
  target/wasm32-unknown-unknown/release/verify.wasm. Like omsim's libverify.wasm, it exports the functions plus
  malloc and free, but it needs no imports.

It measures every metric omsim does at victory (@V), including "product N ..." and "cycle N ...", and supports the
wrong-output checks. It rejects what omsim rejects when loading a solution, with omsim's messages. Not supported:
- Anything about a solution running forever (@∞: throughput, "per repetition ...", "steady state ...", the visual loop
  and the output intervals), since omclone has no loop detection. These return -1 with a "metric" error.
- omsim's limit on collision checks: only the cycle limit (150,000 by default) bounds a run.

Results differ from omsim's where omclone follows the game (see OVERLAP.md), and where omsim treats extending a
non-piston arm or moving an arm off track as a crash. Crash messages are omclone's own. "number of atoms" counts every
atom, where omsim counts one per hex. Like omsim, the library doesn't check its arguments: a null verifier stops the
process (only verifier_disable_limits accepts one).

verify/compare.py checks the library through its C API: "diff" compares every @V metric with omsim's library over a
folder of solutions, and "leaderboard" checks the leaderboard's recorded scores (see its usage text). Build omsim's
library with "make libverify.so", or on Windows (with MinGW) "gcc -O2 -std=c11 -shared -o libverify.dll collision.c
decode.c parse.c sim.c steady-state.c verifier.c -lm".

